Below is a quick‑start guide for reading a JSON file with **`System.Text.Json`** – the built‑in high‑performance JSON library that ships with .NET 5/6/7+.  
I’ll cover the most common patterns (strongly‑typed deserialization, stream‑based reading, async vs sync, and a “dynamic” approach with `JsonDocument`).

---

## 1. The simplest case: deserialize a *known* JSON shape

Suppose you have a file `config.json` that looks like this:

```json
{
  "AppName": "MyCoolApp",
  "MaxRetries": 5,
  "Logging": {
    "Level": "Debug",
    "FilePath": "logs/app.log"
  }
}
```

### 1.1. Define POCOs that match the JSON

```csharp
public class LoggingOptions
{
    public string Level { get; set; } = default!;
    public string FilePath { get; set; } = default!;
}

public class AppConfig
{
    public string AppName { get; set; } = default!;
    public int MaxRetries { get; set; }
    public LoggingOptions Logging { get; set; } = default!;
}
```

### 1.2. Read the file and deserialize

#### Sync, using a `FileStream`

```csharp
using System.IO;
using System.Text.Json;

string path = "config.json";

AppConfig config;
using (var stream = File.OpenRead(path))
{
    config = JsonSerializer.Deserialize<AppConfig>(stream)!;
}
```

> **Why a stream?**  
> `File.OpenRead` gives you a `FileStream` that is *already* in binary form (UTF‑8 by default).  
> `JsonSerializer.Deserialize<T>(Stream)` is a fully synchronous method that reads directly from the stream – no intermediate string allocation.

#### Sync, using `File.ReadAllText`

If the file is small and you prefer the “read‑all‑text” style:

```csharp
string json = File.ReadAllText(path);
AppConfig config = JsonSerializer.Deserialize<AppConfig>(json)!;
```

> This is convenient but creates an extra `string` in memory.

#### Async

```csharp
using System.Text.Json;
using System.IO;

AppConfig config = await JsonSerializer.DeserializeAsync<AppConfig>(File.OpenRead(path));
```

> `DeserializeAsync<T>` is great for I/O‑bound workloads (e.g., a web API that reads a configuration file on start‑up).

---

## 2. Customizing the deserialization

You can tweak the default behavior with `JsonSerializerOptions`. For example, to use camel‑case property names, ignore null values, or support `enum` string values:

```csharp
var options = new JsonSerializerOptions
{
    PropertyNamingPolicy = JsonNamingPolicy.CamelCase,  // JSON: "maxRetries" → C#: MaxRetries
    WriteIndented = true,                               // nice formatting for debugging
    AllowTrailingCommas = true,
    ReadCommentHandling = JsonCommentHandling.Skip,
    Converters = { new JsonStringEnumConverter() }      // enums as strings
};

AppConfig config = JsonSerializer.Deserialize<AppConfig>(json, options)!;
```

---

## 3. When you don’t know the schema (or you only need a few values)

Sometimes you just want to peek at a value, or the JSON shape is dynamic. `System.Text.Json` offers two lightweight “dynamic” APIs:

### 3.1. `JsonDocument` (read‑only, tree‑like)

```csharp
using System.Text.Json;

using var document = JsonDocument.Parse(json);
JsonElement root = document.RootElement;

// Example: read the "AppName" property
string appName = root.GetProperty("AppName").GetString()!;

// Example: read an array
foreach (var item in root.GetProperty("Servers").EnumerateArray())
{
    string host = item.GetProperty("Host").GetString()!;
    int port = item.GetProperty("Port").GetInt32();
    // ...
}
```

> `JsonDocument` is perfect for fast, one‑off reads. It keeps the JSON in a memory‑efficient, read‑only representation.

### 3.2. `Utf8JsonReader` (low‑level, streaming)

If you want to process a huge file without allocating a whole object graph:

```csharp
using System.IO;
using System.Text.Json;

using var stream = File.OpenRead(path);
var buffer = new byte[8192];
int bytesRead;

while ((bytesRead = stream.Read(buffer)) > 0)
{
    var reader = new Utf8JsonReader(buffer.AsSpan(0, bytesRead));
    while (reader.Read())
    {
        if (reader.TokenType == JsonTokenType.PropertyName &&
            reader.ValueTextEquals("AppName"))
        {
            reader.Read(); // move to the value
            string appName = reader.GetString()!;
            Console.WriteLine($"AppName: {appName}");
            break; // or continue walking the stream
        }
    }
}
```

> `Utf8JsonReader` gives you the ultimate control and lowest memory footprint, but it’s more verbose.

---

## 4. Full example: put it all together

```csharp
using System;
using System.IO;
using System.Text.Json;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

public class Program
{
    static async Task Main()
    {
        string path = "config.json";

        // 1. Strongly‑typed deserialization (async)
        var config = await JsonSerializer.DeserializeAsync<AppConfig>(File.OpenRead(path));
        Console.WriteLine($"App: {config.AppName}, Retries: {config.MaxRetries}");
        Console.WriteLine($"Log level: {config.Logging.Level}");

        // 2. Quick peek with JsonDocument
        var json = await File.ReadAllTextAsync(path);
        using var doc = JsonDocument.Parse(json);
        Console.WriteLine($"File path (from JsonDocument): {doc.RootElement.GetProperty("Logging").GetProperty("FilePath").GetString()}");

        // 3. Low‑level stream parsing (optional)
        // ... (see section 3.2 above)
    }
}
```

---

## 5. Quick FAQ

| Question | Answer |
|----------|--------|
| **Do I need `using System.Text.Json`?** | Yes, that namespace contains `JsonSerializer`. |
| **Can I use `JsonSerializerOptions` for custom property names?** | Absolutely – `PropertyNamingPolicy`, `DictionaryKeyPolicy`, custom `JsonConverter`s. |
| **Is `JsonSerializer.Deserialize<T>(Stream)` thread‑safe?** | The serializer itself is thread‑safe; each call uses its own instance of `Utf8JsonReader`, so you can call it concurrently. |
| **What about backwards compatibility with older .NET?** | `System.Text.Json` is available from .NET 5 onward. For .NET Core 3.1 you can use the preview package, or fall back to `Newtonsoft.Json`. |
| **Do I need to close the stream?** | Yes, wrap the `FileStream` in a `using` or `await using` block to ensure disposal. |

---

### TL;DR

```csharp
using System.Text.Json;
using System.IO;

var options = new JsonSerializerOptions { PropertyNamingPolicy = JsonNamingPolicy.CamelCase };

var config = await JsonSerializer.DeserializeAsync<AppConfig>(File.OpenRead("config.json"), options);
```

That’s all there is to it! Happy coding.
